<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Karlskirche</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1716">1716</a>–<a href="/years/1737">1737</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Karlskirche is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a> church in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> dedicated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-borromeo">Saint Charles Borromeo</a>, built by Emperor Charles VI in fulfilment of a vow made during the plague of 1713. Designed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johann-bernhard-fischer-von-erlach">Johann Bernhard Fischer von Erlach</a> and completed by his son Joseph Emanuel between 1716 and 1737, it combines a great dome with a classical portico and two columns modelled on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trajans-column">Trajan's Column</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, expressing imperial and Catholic claims.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/karlskirche.jpg" alt="Karlskirche" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Thomas Ledl · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Karlskirche_Abendsonne_1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
